    "result_text": "The build passes. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nRejecting a new owner whose lower-cased email already belongs to another owner, responding **409 Conflict** \u2014 following the existing \"ensure unique\" pattern already used for telephone and household.\n\n**New files:**\n\n1. `EnsureUniqueEmail.java` \u2014 a pipeline function that reads the shared `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, skips when the email is null/blank (email is optional), and otherwise compares its lower-cased value against every existing owner's lower-cased email, throwing the checked `DuplicateEmailException` on a collision.\n\n2. `DuplicateEmailException.java` \u2014 a checked exception carrying the conflicting email (mirrors `DuplicateTelephoneException`).\n\n3. `DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 maps the exception to a 409 `ProblemDetail` with the `email` property (mirrors the telephone handler).\n\n4. `.../escalation/...DuplicateEmailException.yml` \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n**Wiring:** inserted `ensureUniqueEmail` into `owners.POST.yml` right after `normalizeEmail` (so the check runs on the already-lower-cased canonical value) and before `build`, under the same `transaction` governance as its neighbours.\n\nThe single-responsibility split keeps normalization (`NormalizeOwnerEmail`) separate from the uniqueness rule, and reuses the established exception/handler/escalation idiom rather than adding logic to an existing class.\n\nThe provided acceptance test `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
